                  <origin>
                     <origDate when-custom="128X/129X" notBefore="1280" notAfter="1299" calendar="#gregorian">s. xiii ex.</origDate>
                  </origin>
                  <provenance type="#e"><orgName role="fmo" key="mlgbOrg_426">Oxford, Oxfordshire.
                        Balliol College (Domus de Balliolo)</orgName><p>‘At the head of 1v, in the
                        same hand as a similar entry in MS 42, is Ista secunda pars secunde partis
                        summe fratris Th. de Aquino . . . et constitit viij libr. paris. id est l
                        sol. sterlingorum. No doubt, therefore, this volume came with MS 42 from
                        William Burnell, and on 3 (erased) is the late 14th-cent. College
                        inventory-mark 29’ liber theologie. A pledge-note, early 15th cent., is on
                        1: primum supplementum . . . . Some time in the first half of the century it
                        must have gone astray like MS 42, for on 1 is: Istum librum legavit Magister
                        Willelmus Duffeld ad usum sociorum collegii | de Baliolo Oxonie et dedit eis
                        eundem ad instanciam Magistri Willelmi Moreton | quondam socii eiusdem domus
                        anno regis Henrici sexti xxxj. mo s&lt;cilicet&gt; dedit (Duffield, who was
                        a fellow of Merton Coll., died 7 March 1453; Emden i. 601). On 2v is a late
                        15th-cent. Balliol title: 2a 2e Sancti Thome.’ (Mynors, Balliol,
                     31)</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">William Burnell (d. 1304), dean of
                     WellsWilliam Duffield (d. 1453), fellow of Merton</provenance>
